main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ trivially copyable at run time: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [class.prop]/1, [basic.types.general]/9: a class is trivially
copyable when its copy/move constructors, assignment operators and
destructor are all trivial (or deleted, with one non-deleted); a
bit-field member does not affect that.  The built-in
__is_trivially_copyable(T) was false for any class with a bit-field
(`struct bits { int a : 3; };').  libc++'s <format> asserts
`is_trivially_copyable_v<__parsed_specifications<char>>'
(parser_std_format_spec.h:324/327; the struct has a bit-field in an
anonymous union); the failing static_asserts were dropped as
system-header declarations in 11 libc++ C++20 tests ("dropped 3
system-header declarations").  test_libcxx.desc is such a program and
forbids only those two drops.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept both
programs.  A bit-field member is now judged by its
underlying type.  Fails on bin53.
